        "content": "{\"formal\": {\"statement\": \"∀ (p : ℕ)\\n(F : ℕ → ℕ)\\n(poddprime : Odd p ∧ p.Prime)\\n(hF : ∀ n : ℕ, F n = ∑ i ∈ Finset.range (p - 1), (i + 1) * n ^ i), ∀ a ∈ Finset.Icc 1 p, ∀ b ∈ Finset.Icc 1 p, a ≠ b → ¬(F a ≡ F b [MOD p])\", \"namespace\": \"\", \"supporting_declarations\": [], \"rendered\": \"∀ (p : ℕ) (F : (ℕ → ℕ)),\\n  (((Odd p) ∧ (Nat.Prime p)) →\\n    ((∀ (n : ℕ), ((F n) = (∑ i ∈ (Finset.range (p - 1)), ((i + 1) * (n ^ i))))) →\\n      (∀ a ∈ (Finset.Icc 1 p), (∀ b ∈ (Finset.Icc 1 p), ((a ≠ b) → ¬((F a) ≡ (F b) [MOD p]))))))\"}, \"signature\": \"(p : ℕ)\\n(F : ℕ → ℕ)\\n(poddprime : Odd p ∧ p.Prime)\\n(hF : ∀ n : ℕ, F n = ∑ i ∈ Finset.range (p - 1), (i + 1) * n ^ i)\\n: ∀ a ∈ Finset.Icc 1 p, ∀ b ∈ Finset.Icc 1 p, a ≠ b → ¬(F a ≡ F b [MOD p])\", \"informal\": \"Let $p$ be in the set $\\\\{3,5,7,11,\\\\dots\\\\}$ of odd primes and let $F(n)=1+2n+3n^2+\\\\dots+(p-1)n^{p-2}$. Prove that if $a$ and $b$ are distinct integers in $\\\\{0,1,2,\\\\dots,p-1\\\\}$ then $F(a)$ and $F(b)$ are not congruent modulo $p$, that is, $F(a)-F(b)$ is not exactly divisible by $p$.\", \"docstring\": \"/--\\nLet $p$ be in the set $\\\\{3,5,7,11,\\\\dots\\\\}$ of odd primes and let $F(n)=1+2n+3n^2+\\\\dots+(p-1)n^{p-2}$. Prove that if $a$ and $b$ are distinct integers in $\\\\{0,1,2,\\\\dots,p-1\\\\}$ then $F(a)$ and $F(b)$ are not congruent modulo $p$, that is, $F(a)-F(b)$ is not exactly divisible by $p$.\\n-/\", \"prechecks\": {\"status\": \"completed\", \"hints\": [{\"kind\": \"natural_arithmetic_review\", \"status\": \"advisory\"}], \"vacuity\": \"unresolved\"}, \"advisory\": null}"
